<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Learned About the History of Panama.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain cultural concepts or idioms to others. Text: Summarize complex texts for peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain basic historical and cultural concepts, using visuals and simple definitions (e.g., "Independence means freedom from another country," while showing a timeline on the board). Can summarize key points from a history text by listing dates and events on the board (e.g., "1903: Separation of Panama from Colombia" and "1914: Canal Completed"). Can help others understand historical terms with gestures and pointing (e.g., "Colony" as "when another country is in control," with a map).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> "Historical Headlines." The teacher displays images of key Panamanian historical events (e.g., separation in 1903, Canal construction, Canal transfer). Students work in pairs to recall and briefly explain one event to their partner, focusing on summarizing the main idea. Teacher prompts: "What was a significant *event* in Panama's *history* we *learned* about? Can you *summarize* its *significance* in one sentence?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to summarize and explain a historical event simply. Example: "Panama *declared* its *independence* from Colombia in *1903*. This *transformed* its *identity* and led to the *construction* of the *canal*." Teacher models pronunciation of '-ed' endings in 'declared,' 'transformed,' 'completed,' and contrastive sounds /s/ in 'say' vs. /z/ in 'was'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> "What does it mean to 'summarize' something for someone else?" (Expected: 'to give the main points briefly'). "Why is it important to use clear language when explaining *history*?" (Expected: 'so others can *understand*').</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt from a simplified historical report about the *construction* and early *significance* of the Panama Canal. The text uses vocabulary such as *engineering, marvel, transformation, economy, routes, legacy*. It includes examples of the passive voice ("The canal *was completed* in *1914*") and reported speech ("Historians say the *canal transformed* global *economy*").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will listen/read to understand complex historical information to prepare for summarizing and explaining it to others.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gist: "What is the main *period* of Panamanian *history* described in this text?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: Students read statements about the text and determine if they are true or false (e.g., "The *canal was completed* before *1900*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Information Matching: Students match historical dates (e.g., *1914*) with key *events* (e.g., *canal completed*).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice summarizing texts and explaining concepts using Past Simple, Reported Speech, and Passive Voice. Vocabulary: *history, Panama, independence, separation, canal, significance, construction, economy, transformation, legacy, heritage*. Pronunciation: Focus on stress for two-syllable nouns/verbs (e.g., "REsearch" vs. "reSEARCH") and /d/, /t/, /ɪd/ for '-ed' endings. Pragmatic: Asking for clarification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Concept Simplification (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides 3-4 complex sentences or terms from the text (e.g., "The *Panama Canal* represented a monumental feat of *engineering*, permanently altering global trade *routes* and fostering unprecedented economic *transformation*."). Students work individually to rewrite or explain these in simpler, clearer terms for a peer who might not *understand* them fully.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Reported History Chain (Semi-controlled):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by stating a historical fact (e.g., "Panama *declared independence* in *1903*."). The next student uses reported speech to relay the information and adds another fact: "She said that Panama *declared independence* in *1903*. *Historians* also *reported* that the *canal was completed* in *1914*." This continues around the circle, practicing reported speech and passive voice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Peer Explanation & Clarification (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a flashcard with a historical term (e.g., *Colony, Revolution, Legacy, Sovereignty*) or a key date (*1903, 1914, 1977*). One student *explains* their term/date to their partner, providing a simple definition or context, using gestures if helpful. The partner listens and asks at least one clarifying question (e.g., "Could you *explain* what 'sovereignty' *means* in simpler words?" or "Why do you *believe* that date is *significant*?") using target pragmatic phrases.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> "Panama History Infographic Explanation Session." Students will present and *explain* their "Panama History Infographic" (the 21st Century Skills Project) to another small group of peers. The goal is to mediate the historical information presented in their infographic clearly and concisely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Format:</b> Small group presentations (3-4 students per group). Students take turns explaining different sections of their infographic to their peers. They must use visuals from their infographic to support their explanations and definitions. They are encouraged to use the Past Simple, Passive Voice, and Reported Speech to describe historical *events* and *significance*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Groups display their infographics.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. One group presents its infographic to another group. Each member of the presenting group takes a turn to *explain* a specific *period* or *event* from their infographic (e.g., "Here, we *summarize* the *separation* of *Panama* from Colombia in *1903*. This *event* *was* crucial because...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Presenters actively define or clarify historical concepts and terms using simple language or gestures (e.g., pointing to a timeline, saying "This *period* *began* when...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The listening group asks questions to *understand* better or for *clarification* (e.g., "What *impact* did the *construction* of the *canal* have on the country's *economy* immediately?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Groups switch roles.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to use phrases like: "This means...", "In other words...", "To put it simply...", "What I'm trying to say is...".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually explain one key historical *event* or *cultural concept* from their "Panama History Infographic" to a peer or the teacher. They must *summarize* the *event* and *explain* its *significance* using clear language, target grammar, and vocabulary. The explanation should be concise, around 1-2 minutes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation/Peer Feedback):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and intelligibility of explanation (Pronunciation: focusing on /s/ vs. /z/, /ð/ vs. /θ/, and -ed endings).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective *summarization* of historical *events* and concepts (using Past Simple and Passive Voice).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate and appropriate use of target vocabulary (*independence, canal, transformation, legacy, heritage*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to *explain* concepts/terms (e.g., *colonial, revolution*) using simple language, definitions, or visuals/gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active engagement in group discussions and willingness to *clarify* (Sociolinguistic and Pragmatic Competence).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One historical *event* or concept I *explained* well today was...</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One *cultural concept* or *historical term* I want to improve explaining next time is...</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did I help my classmates *understand* aspects of *Panama's history* today through my explanations and infographic?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did I face when trying to *summarize* or *explain* complex historical ideas or *cultural concepts* to my peers, and how can I overcome them in the future?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Were students able to effectively mediate historical information from their infographics to their peers? Did they successfully *explain cultural concepts* and *summarize complex texts*? What specific mediation strategies did they use (e.g., simple definitions, gestures, visuals)? Did they utilize the target grammar (Past Simple, Reported Speech, Passive Voice) and vocabulary effectively? What adjustments are needed for future mediation tasks or projects involving research and presentation of information, especially concerning clarity and simplification for a target audience?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> (As this is Lesson 5, the final lesson of the sequence) Students will continue to apply these developed mediation skills in future academic and real-world contexts, particularly when conducting *research*, analyzing *historical* *events*, and presenting complex information clearly and effectively to diverse audiences. The skills of *summarizing*, *explaining*, and *clarifying* are crucial for lifelong learning and effective communication.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>